  <info><![CDATA[# Experiment 0: Dynamic Bandit Base

## PURPOSE

This is the validation model for the later deference experiments. It does not compare Preemption and Total Evidence, and the expert sends no testimony.

## ENVIRONMENT

Arm A has known success probability 0.50. In a B-good world, B succeeds with probability 0.55; in a B-bad world, B succeeds with probability 0.45. The world stays fixed for the full run.

## LEARNING

Agents maintain the log odds that the world is B-good. B success adds ln(0.55 / 0.45), B failure subtracts the same amount, and A outcomes do not change the belief. Binary Thompson sampling selects B with probability equal to the current posterior probability that the world is B-good.

## AGENTS AND NETWORK

There is one expert and ten laypeople. The expert has five sequential private research pulls per tick for the first 25 ticks and then stops. Each layperson has one pull per tick for 500 ticks. Laypeople form a ring and, when peer-sharing? is on, send each first-hand result only to their two immediate neighbors. A result generated in tick t can affect neighbors only after that tick's actions and before tick t+1 actions. Messages are never forwarded.

The central spokes display the authority relation but carry no content in Experiment 0.

## INTERPRETING OUTPUTS

Posterior truth mass is q in B-good and 1-q in B-bad. Expected regret increases by 0.05 whenever an agent chooses the inferior arm. The final-window diagnostic describes erroneous persistence during the last 50 ticks; Thompson sampling has no absorbing lock-in state.

This model can validate dynamic expertise, selective sampling, and local evidence transmission. It cannot support a conclusion about either deference rule.]]></info>

